+# GSA telephone equipment, circuit and regulatory file series
+
+**November 9, 2026 | B129 / PD03–PD05.** National Archives schedules identify specific types of GSA records to seek for the FAA recording question. Historical telephone inventories could list auxiliary equipment and connection dates. Circuit and engineering files could contain completion notices and installation or test reports. A later schedule places telecommunications services in **RG 137** and separately identifies a permanent GSA regulatory case-file item. The actual FAA line records, GSA notification registers, and archival transfer or disposal evidence remain unacquired.
+
+## Records that could test equipment coverage
+
+| Source and item | Observed scope and disposition |
+| --- | --- |
+| **NC1-352-80-01, 55C85** | Telephone inventories list main station numbers, locations, extensions, auxiliary equipment and connection or disconnection details. The schedule distinguishes cards from machine listings and gives **four months** for machine listings. This is a historic inventory description and instruction. It supplies no station entry for the FAA. [Physical p. 14, lines 30–44](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0352/nc1-352-80-01_sf115.pdf#page=14) |
+| **NC1-352-80-01, 55G1** | Communications service authorization files include design and configuration, service requests, installation, change or disconnection records, and completion notices. The described cutoff follows disconnection or termination, with annual cutoff, one-year hold and retirement to the Federal Records Center (FRC). The face says **“Destroy when 5 years old.”** [Physical p. 33, lines 26–39](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0352/nc1-352-80-01_sf115.pdf#page=33) |
+| **N1-269-96-002, 04C025(a), (c)** | The Office of Federal Telecommunications 2000 schedule describes requirements and engineering project records. It lists acceptance notifications in (a) and installation, modification, test and inspection records in (c). Both items specify an FRC route and **five years after cutoff**, with different inactive-file triggers. The (a) margin references earlier **55B5**. The historical **55B5** text organizes files by agency or entity, then location, organization or subject, and says **“Destroy when 6 years old.”** Keep the two instructions and their time bases as printed. No individual schedule application or disposal has been verified. [1996 schedule, physical p. 2 lines 8–23 and margin, p. 3 lines 1–17](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0269/n1-269-96-002_sf115.pdf#page=2) · [Earlier schedule, p. 7 lines 14–36](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0352/nc1-352-80-01_sf115.pdf#page=7) |
+
+Both historical schedule indexes display **Inactive** for the jobs above. The NC1-352-80-01 cover reports a 2016–2019 rescheduling and lists several successor authorities. **DAA-0352-2016-0001** says telecommunications services transferred to **RG 137**, and separates services for GSA itself from services to other agencies. Its statement concerns services and record-group context. Transfer of particular FAA inventory cards or approval files would need its own documentation. The RG 137 online schedule title screen found no title-level hit for telecommunications, FTS or voice. The separate RG 352 published guide gives **1957–78** for its described holdings, which is narrower than the periods of the schedule documents. An item-level accession or transfer record is still needed. [NC1-352-80-01 cover, lines 2–16](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0352/nc1-352-80-01_sf115.pdf#page=1) · [DAA-0352-2016-0001, physical p. 1 lines 29–35](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0352/daa-0352-2016-0001_sf115.pdf#page=1) · [RG 137 schedules](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/index.html?dir=%2Findependent-agencies%2Frg-0137) · [RG 352 holdings guide](https://www.archives.gov/research/guide-fed-records/groups/352.html)
+
+## Permanent regulatory files and limits of the cross-reference
+
+- **Item 030, DAA-0269-2016-0012-0004.** The schedule describes federal regulatory case files, including changes, correspondence and annual master copies. Its examples include FMR, FTR, FAR and the Federal Real and Personal Property report. The item is marked **Permanent** and **Active**, with transfer **15 years after cutoff** and **1976–2002** in the planned initial-transfer span field. The text layer gives `DM-0269-2016-0012-0004` while the page image reads `DAA-0269-2016-0012-0004`. The item description leaves FIRMR inclusion, accession and actual contents open. The schedule PDF body has **10 physical pages**, with visible footer **Page 6 of 11** and text-layer labels from 1 to 10 of 11. The complete original and the reason for the count difference remain unverified. [Physical pp. 6–7, p. 6 lines 43–57, p. 7 lines 6–34](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0269/daa-0269-2016-0012_sf115.pdf#page=6)
+- **One specific predecessor checked.** Item 030 lists **N1-269-90-004 / 16A30/a**. The index calls that job **Accounting Division**. Its physical p. 6 gives **16A30** as real property inventory reports and **a. Record copy** as permanent, with transfer **10 years after cutoff**. The real-property subject also appears in the later description. The link establishes a predecessor reference to a particular item, with no verified match to recording determinations. Keep the historic and later transfer instructions separate. The other item 030 predecessor citations remain open. [Earlier schedule, physical p. 6 lines 4–19](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0269/n1-269-90-004_sf115.pdf#page=6) · [RG 269 schedule index](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/index.html?dir=%2Findependent-agencies%2Frg-0269)
+- **Separate publication and communications files.** N1-269-92-001 item 17B5 concerns publication development, distribution and costs. DAA-0269-2016-0007 describes the GSA communications program and prepared communications. Those descriptions provide scope checks against treating a generic title match as a telephone-circuit file or a regulatory case docket. [1992 schedule, p. 2 lines 66–74](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0269/n1-269-92-001_sf115.pdf#page=2) · [Communications schedule, p. 1 lines 16–29](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/independent-agencies/rg-0269/daa-0269-2016-0007_sf115.pdf#page=1)
+
+## Next discriminating records
+
+1. Find an **actual 55G1 or 04C025 project/circuit entry**, acceptance or test report, or **55C85** entry keyed to an FAA WOCC station or circuit and date. Compare line identity and capability with the OIG's reported pre-September 11 gap. An engineering record could test capability, while a call-specific record would be required to establish recording of a particular call. No line-level join was performed. [OIG, physical p. 11 lines 11–18](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=11)
+2. Find the records-series transfer, item crosswalk and dated **FRC accession or disposition records**. Separately seek the actual GSA KMPR/KMR/KAR incoming notices and index, and the regulation docket and annual master copies for § 201–21.603. A rulemaking docket and a notice sent under a rule are different record types. [Prior GSA notice comparison](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/0d47d7caf7d85194a7d4e0a55473f3f286a660b6/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-gsa-advance-notice-records-2026-11-09/README.md)
+3. Keep the corrected May 2003 FAA answer and delivery acknowledgment, signed administrative decision, and recommendation responses and closure records as the priority case documents. Historic schedule instructions alone establish neither actual preservation nor destruction of any case record.
+
+The review retained 14 official response bodies, including nine PDF schedules. Inspection was limited to selected schedule entries, index and guide fields, fixed text anchors and eight page-image crops. The signed case decision and five-track completion criteria remain open. [Source pins](SOURCE-PINS.json) · [Additive text diff](TEXT-CHANGES.diff)
